    {
        "id": "why-show-work-if-answer-can-be-wrong",
        "question": "Why does showing the work matter if the answer can still be wrong?",
        "response": {
            "action": "message",
            "message": "Because the work is often more valuable than the final answer.\n\nShowing the work matters even when the answer is wrong because it lets people see:\n\n- **Where the mistake happened** — a wrong answer with clear steps is easier to debug than a guess.\n- **Whether the reasoning was sound up to a point** — you may be right about most of the path and wrong at one step.\n- **How to improve** — others can give feedback on the exact step that failed.\n- **Trust and accountability** — a result with no reasoning is hard to evaluate.\n- **Learning** — in math, writing, science, and decision-making, the process is often the part that teaches.\n\nSo the point of showing work is not “the answer must be right.” It’s “the thinking should be inspectable.” A wrong but transparent solution is usually more useful than a right answer with no explanation, because it can be checked, corrected, and reused.\n\nIf you want, I can also explain this in the context of school, AI, or real-world decision-making.",
