    {
        "id": "what-should-people-know-about-ai-answer",
        "question": "What should people be able to know about an AI answer?",
        "response": {
            "action": "message",
            "message": "People should be able to know, at minimum:\n\n- **Where the answer came from** — whether it’s based on the model’s training, a retrieved source, a calculation, or a guess.\n- **How confident it is** — including uncertainty, likely failure points, and whether there are competing interpretations.\n- **What assumptions it depends on** — especially if changing one detail would change the answer.\n- **What it might be missing** — gaps in data, time sensitivity, ambiguity, or limits in the model’s knowledge.\n- **Whether it was checked** — for example, whether facts were verified against sources or not.\n- **Any relevant limitations or bias** — such as incomplete coverage, outdated information, or perspective effects.\n- **What parts are reasoning vs. fact** — so people can tell inference from evidence.\n- **When human judgment is still needed** — especially for legal, medical, financial, safety, or high-stakes decisions.\n\nA good AI answer should be understandable, traceable, and honest about uncertainty—not just fluent.",
            "modality": "text",
